      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which the points \\((x, y)\\) lie, we need to express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of a single variable. The given points are:\n\\[\n(x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\n\\]\nWe start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\n\\]\n\nTo find the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), we can manipulate these expressions. First, let's express \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) from the first equation:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\implies x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(t\\) into the second equation for \\(y\\):\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{x - 1} - 1}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the numerator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x - 1} - 1 = \\frac{1 - (x - 1)}{x - 1} = \\frac{1 - x + 1}{x - 1} = \\frac{2 - x}{x - 1}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute back into the equation for \\(y\\):\n\\[\ny = \\frac{\\frac{2 - x}{x - 1}}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}} = \\frac{2 - x}{1}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\ny = 1 - x\n\\]\n\nThus, we have found that \\(y\\) is equal to \\(1 - x\\). This means that every point \\((x, y)\\) on the curve satisfies the equation:\n\\[\ny = 1 - x\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a straight line. Thus, the correct answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{A}\n\\]",
