      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which the points \\((x, y)\\) lie, we will solve the system of equations:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t},\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t - 1}{t}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we will express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\implies x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we will express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\implies y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}.\n\\]\n\nNow we have the following equations:\n\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t},\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}.\n\\]\n\nWe can observe the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}.\n\\]\n\nThis equation represents a hyperbola, as we will show.\n\nWe will first substitute \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = -2\\) into the equation \\(y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\nWhen \\(x = 0\\),\n\\[\ny = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = -1 \\implies t = -1.\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(x = -2\\),\n\\[\ny = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = -2 \\implies t = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow, let's substitute \\(y = 0\\) and \\(y = -2\\) into the equation \\(x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\nWhen \\(y = 0\\),\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}.\n\\]\n \nWhen \\(y = -2\\),\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}.\n\\]\n\nTo check if the generated points lie on a curve, we assign the following values for \\(t\\):\n\n\\(x = 0 \\to y = 0\\),\n\\(x = -2 \\to y = -2\\).\n\nIn conclusion, the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) coordinates always maintain the specified relations, hence confirming that the plotted points all lie on a hyperbola as described by the equation:\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}, \\quad y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is answered as (E) Hyperbola. \\(\\boxed{E}\\)",
